(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with recurrent urinary tract infections was treated for pyelonephritis a month ago. She now has low-grade fevers, fatigue and a new murmur, and blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci. Which organism is most likely?","choices":["Staphylococcus epidermidis","Enterococci","Viridans streptococci","Streptococcus gallolyticus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The genitourinary tract is the source of enterococci, which cause subacute and native valve endocarditis; urinary tract infection gives them a route into the blood. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with advanced dilated cardiomyopathy has refractory symptoms on full medical therapy. Severe lung disease and frailty rule him out as a transplant candidate, and he wants to remain at home and mobile. Which treatment option fits his situation?","choices":["Assist device as a bridge to transplant","Assist device as destination therapy","Surgical myectomy of the septum","Urgent listing for cardiac transplant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Destination therapy with a left ventricular assist device is for patients who are not candidates for cardiac transplantation. Patients may live more than five years with the device and can stay mobile. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 19-year-old swimmer has an electrocardiogram before a sports physical. She has no symptoms. The rate is 72, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, but the beats come closer together as she breathes in and spread apart as she breathes out. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus arrhythmia","Sick sinus syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A normal P wave before every narrow QRS at 60 to 100, with the rate rising on inspiration and falling on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia: a normal, physiologic rhythm that is irregular with a pattern. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which medication is a recognized cause of pericardial effusion?","choices":["Amlodipine","Metformin","Doxorubicin","Levothyroxine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medication causes of pericardial effusion include doxorubicin, minoxidil, phenytoin, and hydralazine and procainamide through drug-induced lupus. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Stable atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response is treated how?","choices":["Immediate ablation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous atropine","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A hemodynamically stable patient in rapid ventricular response gets intravenous rate control; a stable patient without it gets rate control by mouth. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman in stable, rate-controlled atrial flutter of unknown duration is to have elective outpatient cardioversion. Which must come first?","choices":["Catheter ablation","21 days of anticoagulation","An adenosine challenge","A permanent pacemaker"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient cardioversion of atrial flutter is done after 21 days of anticoagulation, and anticoagulation is part of flutter management. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 2 weeks with a peripherally inserted central line. She develops fever, and two separate blood cultures grow a gram-positive coccus with no other source. Which organism causes the largest share of this setting's native valve endocarditis?","choices":["Enterococci","Coagulase-negative staphylococci","Staphylococcus aureus","Viridans streptococci"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus causes about 52% of health care-associated native valve endocarditis, and methicillin resistance is common among these strains. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"An elevated D-dimer implies increased activity of which process?","choices":["Fibrinolysis","Platelet production","Red cell destruction","Vitamin K depletion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An elevated D-dimer implies increased fibrinolysis, the breakdown of fibrin clot; it can occur in a deep vein thrombosis but also in many other conditions. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman had a bioprosthetic aortic valve placed 5 months ago. She now has a fever of 39.0 °C (102.2 °F) and a new regurgitant murmur, and blood cultures have been drawn. Which empiric regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Ceftriaxone alone","Vancomycin plus ceftriaxone","Penicillin G plus gentamicin","Vancomycin, gentamicin and cefepime"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When a prosthetic valve has been in place for 1 year or less, empiric therapy is vancomycin plus gentamicin plus cefepime. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has an asymptomatic infrarenal abdominal aortic aneurysm measuring 3.4 cm on ultrasound. Which follow-up is most appropriate?","choices":["No further imaging","Elective repair now","Imaging every 6 to 12 months","Ultrasound every three years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once identified, an aneurysm is followed for size and growth; a 3.0 to 3.9 cm aneurysm is imaged with ultrasound every three years. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman received mediastinal radiation and chemotherapy for a chest cancer 15 years ago. She now has progressive exercise intolerance, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), ascites and swollen legs. Echocardiogram shows stiff ventricles of normal size, marked atrial enlargement and abnormal diastolic function with a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute myocarditis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Dilated cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mediastinal radiation and chemotherapy cause inflammation and then fibrosis that restricts filling, and a prior history of radiation or chemotherapy is a named clue in restrictive cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 182 with palpitations and mild anxiety. Her blood pressure is 128/80, oxygen saturation 98%, and she is alert without chest pain. Which treatment should be tried first?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Intravenous amiodarone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine (up to twice), keeping synchronized cardioversion for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with sudden leg ischemia has sensory loss extending beyond the toes with rest pain, mild-to-moderate weakness, an inaudible arterial Doppler signal and an audible venous signal. Where should his evaluation and treatment take place?","choices":["After outpatient duplex imaging","Nowhere; plan major amputation","After computed tomography first","In the surgical suite, without delay"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An immediately threatened limb is salvageable only with immediate revascularization, so it should preferentially undergo further evaluation and treatment in a surgical suite. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man will finish 5 more weeks of intravenous antibiotics for endocarditis after discharge. Which consultant manages his antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Infectious disease","Addiction medicine","Dermatology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Infectious disease manages antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring pending disposition, as part of the multidisciplinary team. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has taken ibuprofen and colchicine for 8 days for presumed viral pericarditis. She still has a temperature of 38.5 degrees Celsius and pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) pain, and a new pericardial effusion has appeared on echocardiogram. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue the same drugs for a month","Start anakinra as a first step","Admit and look for another cause","Stop colchicine and add a diuretic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Persistent fever and pain with a new effusion define treatment failure, and failure to respond within 1 week suggests a cause other than idiopathic or viral; no improvement after 7 days favors hospitalization. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"When is the dental prophylaxis dose given?","choices":["Daily for a month","30 to 60 minutes before","One week before","The evening after"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The single prophylactic dose is given 30-60 minutes before the procedure, so the drug is circulating when the transient bacteremia occurs. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man arrives with a temperature of 40.2 °C (104.4 °F), rigors and a new murmur, and he looks toxic. Acute endocarditis is suspected. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Antibiotics first, cultures tomorrow","Two cultures within 1-2 hours, then antibiotics","Echocardiography before any cultures","Observation for 24 hours without treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If the presentation suggests acute endocarditis, two cultures are drawn within the first 1-2 hours, and empiric antibiotics are started only after the cultures are drawn. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man collapses in a gym. He is unresponsive, not breathing and has no pulse. The monitor shows chaotic, irregular waves of varying size with no identifiable QRS complexes. Which rhythm is present?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Asystole","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia","Pulseless electrical activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Uncoordinated electrical activity with no rate, no P waves and no organized QRS, in a pulseless patient, is ventricular fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with multiple serious medical conditions is a poor surgical candidate. He had a transient ischemic attack and has a 90% symptomatic carotid stenosis. Which intervention is most appropriate?","choices":["Aspirin and observation alone","Carotid angioplasty with stenting","Digital subtraction angiography alone","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carotid angioplasty with stenting, most often through the common femoral artery, is less invasive and allows intervention in poor surgical candidates with a qualifying stenosis. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman notices occasional skipped beats during a stressful month of heavy coffee and wine. Her electrocardiogram shows occasional early beats with an abnormally shaped P wave and a narrow QRS, and her examination and echocardiogram are normal. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Start anticoagulation","Start a beta blocker","Reassure; avoid triggers","Refer for ablation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Premature atrial contractions in a normal heart with excess alcohol, stress or caffeine are benign: counsel avoidance of precipitants and reassure about the benign nature of the rhythm. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 17-year-old basketball player collapses briefly during a game and recovers. In the emergency department he is in sinus rhythm at 78. His electrocardiogram shows a PR interval of 0.10 seconds and a delta wave (a slurred upstroke at the start of the QRS) with a widened QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Wolff-Parkinson-White","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Sick sinus syndrome","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A short PR interval with a delta wave and widened QRS in sinus rhythm is pre-excitation from an accessory pathway (the bundle of Kent): Wolff-Parkinson-White, which classically first shows itself as collapse in a young athlete. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which disorder in the differential of infective endocarditis causes thromboemboli and cardiac valve disease?","choices":["Lyme disease","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Antiphospholipid syndrome","Meningitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antiphospholipid syndrome causes thromboemboli and cardiac valve disease, so it can imitate the embolic picture of infective endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a large anterior myocardial infarction two years ago has palpitations and lightheadedness. He is alert, with a blood pressure of 112/70. The monitor shows a regular wide QRS rhythm at 170 per minute with no P waves, every complex the same shape, for over a minute. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Complete heart block","Monomorphic ventricular tachycardia","Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A regular wide QRS rhythm of 100 to 250 per minute without P waves, with identical complexes lasting over 30 seconds, is sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia, often seen after myocardial infarction. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man admitted with pneumonia develops frequent early, wide QRS beats with no preceding P wave on telemetry, and he feels his heart skipping. His oxygen saturation on the monitor is drifting down. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start intravenous amiodarone","Refer for catheter ablation","Immediate defibrillation","Check blood gas, electrolytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia and electrolyte imbalance are the main causes of premature ventricular contractions, so new frequent beats call for checking oxygenation and electrolytes first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which description fits recurrent pericarditis?","choices":["Symptoms persist without remission for up to 3 months","Symptoms persist without any remission past 3 months","Symptoms return after a symptom-free gap of 4 to 6 weeks","Constriction persists after an effusion has been drained"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent pericarditis is when symptoms of acute pericarditis return after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer, which separates it from incessant disease. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man on telemetry has a prolonged QT after starting a new medication and then develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape and twist around the baseline). He becomes unresponsive and pulseless. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Transcutaneous pacing","Intravenous magnesium first","Defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, including torsades, is treated as cardiac arrest with defibrillation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation; magnesium follows. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What most commonly causes a lower extremity arteriovenous fistula?","choices":["Surgical creation for dialysis","Congenital malformation","Blunt trauma to the thigh","Groin access for a procedure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lower extremity fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic, from using the groin (femoral vessels) for percutaneous arterial and venous access in endovascular procedures. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has her second recurrence of idiopathic pericarditis despite colchicine. Her temperature is 38.4 degrees Celsius, C-reactive protein is 45 mg/L, and cardiac magnetic resonance shows pericardial edema with late gadolinium enhancement. Which therapy is most appropriate to add?","choices":["Pericardiectomy","Intravenous immunoglobulin","Azathioprine","Anakinra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With an inflammatory phenotype (C-reactive protein above 10 mg/L, fever, imaging inflammation) and a colchicine-resistant second recurrence of idiopathic disease, an interleukin-1 blocker such as anakinra or rilonacept is added and now used earlier. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with Mobitz type II block found on a routine tracing declines a pacemaker because he feels perfectly well. Which risk should be explained to him?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation and stroke","Progression to complete block","Easing into first-degree block","Gradual return to normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II carries a higher risk of progression to complete heart block and sudden cardiac death, and it is considered dangerous even without symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which is the first treatment for torsades de pointes in a conscious patient?","choices":["Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with diabetes who smokes wakes most nights with burning pain in his forefoot and toes that eases when he hangs the leg over the bed; he now sleeps in a recliner. Which ankle-brachial index range would be expected?","choices":["0.4 to 0.9","Above 0.9 to 1.3","0 to 0.4","Above 1.3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rest pain and tissue loss (ulcer, gangrene) correspond to an ankle-brachial index of 0 to 0.4, the critical limb ischemia band, well below the claudication range. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 23-year-old previously healthy man has breathlessness, chest pain and exercise intolerance ten days after a flulike illness with fever, myalgia and diarrhea. He is tachycardic with a third heart sound (an early diastolic sound just after the second heart sound) and crackles at both bases. Troponin is elevated and coronary angiography is normal. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute viral myocarditis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Takotsubo cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute heart failure in an otherwise healthy young man with no cardiac history, one to two weeks after a flulike prodrome, with raised troponin and normal coronaries, is acute viral myocarditis. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has a first proximal deep vein thrombosis with no surgery, trauma, hormone use, travel, cancer or other identifiable trigger, and no inherited disorder has been found. How long should anticoagulation continue?","choices":["Six weeks","Three months","Three to six months","Indefinitely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A first venous thromboembolism with no identifiable risk factor is unprovoked, and its source, such as an undiscovered inherited clotting disorder, is unknown. Treatment therefore continues for an indefinite length of time. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman spent 9 days in the hospital for a large pericardial effusion that needed two procedures. She is now weak and needs help to walk. Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service is most appropriate before she goes home?","choices":["Rehabilitation","Genetics","Nephrology","Infectious disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older adults are more likely to need rehabilitation after prolonged illness and procedures, which also carry higher morbidity and mortality at her age. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with knee osteoarthritis felt a sudden sharp pain behind the right knee while climbing stairs, followed by severe calf discomfort and swelling. Venous duplex ultrasound shows no thrombus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Calf muscle tear","Ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst","Post-thrombotic syndrome","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A ruptured popliteal (Baker) cyst produces severe, sudden calf discomfort that mimics deep vein thrombosis, and the ultrasound shows no clot. It is a standard item on the differential of a swollen calf. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is examined by a student who hears a loud systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Respiration does not change it. Which bedside maneuver would soften her murmur?","choices":["Standing up quickly","Valsalva maneuver","Sustained handgrip","Amyl nitrite inhalation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases blood flow in the heart reduces outflow obstruction and softens the murmur. Handgrip raises afterload, and squatting or passive leg raising raise preload, so all three decrease the murmur. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman is recovering in hospital from stress cardiomyopathy that followed news of her son's serious illness. Her chest pain has resolved, and she asks whether her heart will stay damaged. Which answer about her prognosis is most accurate?","choices":["Most patients recover completely","Poorest outlook of any type","Half die within five years","It always progresses to failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Takotsubo is a transient regional systolic dysfunction, and most patients completely recover, which distinguishes its prognosis from the progressive cardiomyopathies. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who smokes and has diabetes reports aching in both buttocks and hips after walking two blocks, relieved by about 10 minutes of rest. He also reports erectile dysfunction. Both femoral pulses are diminished. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Aortoiliac occlusive disease","Lumbar spinal stenosis","Tibial artery disease","Superficial femoral artery disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Buttock and hip claudication points to the aortoiliac segment, and the triad of claudication, absent or diminished femoral pulses and erectile dysfunction is Leriche syndrome, the severe form of aortoiliac disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman has a 1.4 cm femoral pseudoaneurysm on ultrasound after cardiac catheterization. She is comfortable and the limb is well perfused. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Open surgical repair","Watch and wait","Intravenous heparin","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm under 2 cm in diameter may undergo spontaneous thrombosis and regression, so watchful waiting is appropriate. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"What must follow chest or back pain in a patient with a known thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Reassurance and analgesia","An exercise stress test","Routine yearly imaging","Urgent imaging"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chest or back pain with a known or suspected thoracic aortic aneurysm must undergo urgent imaging to rule out the aneurysm as the cause of the pain. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with dilated, tortuous leg veins has a duplex ultrasound confirming superficial venous reflux. Which vein is most commonly involved in this condition?","choices":["Great saphenous vein","Femoral vein","Short saphenous vein","Popliteal vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Varicose veins usually involve the superficial veins, and the great saphenous vein is the most commonly involved, with the short saphenous vein also affected. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man presents with painful blue toes and palpable pedal pulses. On abdominal examination there is a pulsatile mass above the umbilicus. Which lesion is the likely source of his emboli?","choices":["Femoral pseudoaneurysm","Carotid artery plaque","Thoracic aortic dissection","Abdominal aortic aneurysm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Atheroemboli originate from proximal atherosclerotic plaques, and an abdominal aortic aneurysm can itself shed emboli, producing blue toe syndrome as one of its embolic phenomena. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"In sick sinus syndrome, a fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves alternates with pauses. Which rhythm is the fast run?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular fast rhythm with no P waves is atrial fibrillation, and in tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which fast and slow rhythms alternate, atrial fibrillation is the most common tachyarrhythmia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with a normal pulse examination and ankle-brachial index has had her venous ulcer heal after months of compression. She asks why it opened again after she bumped her leg. Which feature of a healed venous ulcer explains this?","choices":["Fragile thin scar tissue","Undertreated deep infection","Lymphatic channel obstruction","Persistent arterial ischemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A venous ulcer heals with a thin scar that breaks down easily with minor trauma or increased leg edema, so recurrence is common without consistent compression. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman had a mechanical mitral valve placed 4 years ago. She has 5 weeks of low-grade fever and fatigue, and blood cultures are pending. Which organism is most likely in her prosthetic valve infection?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Escherichia coli","Staphylococcus epidermidis","Candida albicans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Late prosthetic valve endocarditis is caused by viridans streptococci, while early infection (around surgery) is caused by coagulase-negative staphylococci and Staphylococcus aureus. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer comes to the emergency department with three days of left calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the right, plus tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. He has no history of clots and nothing else explains his leg. The team debates whether to order a D-dimer or go straight to imaging. Using the Wells criteria, which is the best next step?","choices":["D-dimer testing first","Ankle-brachial index","Contrast ascending venography","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, calf swelling over 3 cm and tenderness along the deep veins total 3 points, a high likelihood. A score of 3 or more skips the D-dimer and goes directly to venous duplex ultrasound of the leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with complete heart block is stabilized with transcutaneous pacing in the emergency department. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Oral beta blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Transcutaneous pacing is the emergent bridge; the definitive treatment for complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a cold 10 days ago. He now has sharp, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) substernal chest pain that is worse lying flat and eases when he leans forward. A pericardial friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound) is heard at the left sternal border, and the electrocardiogram shows widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression and ST depression in aVR. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pulmonary embolism","Anterior myocardial infarction","Viral pleuritis","Acute pericarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sharp pleuritic, positional pain after a viral illness, a friction rub and widespread concave ST elevation with PR depression (reciprocal changes in aVR) meet well over two of the four diagnostic criteria for acute pericarditis. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Where do atherosclerotic lesions most often form?","choices":["At branch points and turbulent sites","In the veins draining the lower legs","In straight segments with laminar flow","In the smallest arterioles of the skin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atherosclerotic lesions form at arterial bifurcations (branching) and at sites of increased turbulence, and they develop over many decades before causing stenosis, thrombosis or atheroemboli. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with end-stage dilated cardiomyopathy is listed for cardiac transplantation, but his breathlessness and fatigue worsen despite full medical therapy and a biventricular defibrillator. Which device strategy fits his situation?","choices":["Assist device as a bridge to transplant","Pacemaker for isolated heart block","Assist device as destination therapy","Alcohol ablation of the septum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A left ventricular assist device pumps blood from the left ventricle into the aorta. As bridge therapy it supports a patient awaiting transplantation or waiting for function to return. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with heart failure has runs of 5 identical wide QRS beats on a Holter monitor. He has no symptoms during them. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator now","Synchronized cardioversion","Optimize his heart failure care","Start oral amiodarone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic non-sustained ventricular tachycardia is managed by optimizing treatment of the underlying cardiac comorbidities; it is not by itself an indication for a defibrillator. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man is diagnosed with a first proximal deep vein thrombosis five days after knee replacement under two hours of general anesthesia. His clot is attributed to the venous stasis and endothelial trauma of the Virchow triad, both from the surgery. What duration of anticoagulation is appropriate?","choices":["Indefinitely","Three months","Three to six months","Six weeks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A clot provoked by a major transient, reversible risk factor, such as surgery under general anesthesia for more than 30 minutes, is treated for three months. Three months is also the minimum initial duration for any first venous thromboembolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has been hospitalized for four days receiving intravenous antibiotics through a peripheral catheter in her left forearm. She now has pain, redness and warmth along the course of the vein, and a firm, tender, palpable cord can be felt beneath the skin. She is afebrile, and the redness stays within the line of the vein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis","Upper extremity deep vein thrombosis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tenderness, induration and erythema along a superficial vein, with an indurated palpable cord after recent catheter use, is superficial thrombophlebitis. Redness confined to the vein argues against infection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college athlete has exertional chest pain without coronary disease and a harsh systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Hypertension and aortic valve disease have been excluded. Which echocardiogram finding confirms the clinical diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Apical ballooning and normal septum","Unexplained wall of 1.5 cm or more","Dilated thin walls with low fraction","Pericardial thickening on imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The clinical diagnosis is confirmed when echocardiography shows unexplained left ventricular wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more anywhere in the wall, most often as asymmetric septal hypertrophy with a small cavity. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with lung cancer has a moderate effusion, dyspnea and a blood pressure of 100/68. Her clinician measures her blood pressure through the respiratory cycle. Which result most supports tamponade?","choices":["No change with respiration","A 15 mmHg drop on inspiration","A 15 mmHg rise on inspiration","A 4 mmHg drop on inspiration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulsus paradoxus, a 10 mmHg drop in blood pressure with inspiration, is a physical finding of both acute and subacute tamponade, along with an elevated jugular venous pressure. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 2-month-old infant with a central line has poor feeding, fast breathing, tachycardia and a changing murmur. Blood cultures are positive and echocardiography shows a vegetation. Which complications are particularly common in infants because of septic emboli?","choices":["Glomerulonephritis","Osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia","Heart failure from valve stenosis","Janeway lesions and Roth spots"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Septic emboli are common in neonates and infants, producing osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia, and neurologic signs such as seizures, hemiparesis and apnea. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with recurrent pericarditis over several years now has leg edema, ascites and an elevated jugular venous pressure. Constrictive pericarditis is suspected. Which echocardiogram finding would best support it?","choices":["Right ventricular diastolic collapse","Asymmetric septal hypertrophy","A dilated, weakly contracting ventricle","Septal bounce toward the left ventricle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiographic features of constriction are increased pericardial thickness, abnormal septal motion or bounce toward the left ventricle, pronounced respiratory variation in ventricular filling and moderate biatrial enlargement. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which cardiomyopathy strategy applies specifically to adolescents?","choices":["Genetic screening for hypertrophic disease","Cautious use of diuretics and vasodilators","Palliative care discussions when advanced","Assist device or transplant consideration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For adolescents the strategies are genetic screening for hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and activity restriction for high-risk individuals. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 105"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man becomes hypotensive and short of breath an hour after a catheter procedure perforates his heart. Echocardiogram shows only a small pericardial effusion, estimated at about 150 mL, with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which best explains why so little fluid has compromised him?","choices":["Chronic stretching of the sac","The echocardiogram underestimated a liter","Rapid filling of an unstretched sac","The pericardium was already calcified"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute effusions can produce tamponade with small amounts of fluid (under 200 mL) because the pericardium has no time to stretch, unlike chronic effusions that grow large over time. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is brought in confused, with a blood pressure of 76/40. The monitor shows a slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Adenosine","Atropine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia is treated first with atropine; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
